The most essential Git commands for managing a project are used to initialize repositories, track changes, and synchronize work with others. Here is a breakdown of basic Git commands categorized by their function:

1. Getting Startedgit init: Initializes a brand new local repository in your current directory.git clone <url>: Creates a local copy of an existing remote repository from a URL.git config --global user.name "Name": Sets your name for attribution in your commits.git config --global user.email "email@example.com": Sets your email for attribution

2. The Daily Workflow (Stage & Commit)git status: Shows the current state of your working directory, including which files are modified or untracked.git add <file>: Stages specific changes for the next commit. Use git add . to stage all changes in the current directory.git commit -m "message": Saves a snapshot of your staged changes with a descriptive message.git log: Displays the history of commits for the current branch

3. Branching & Merginggit branch: Lists all local branches in your repository.git checkout <branch-name>: Switches from the current branch to another branch.git checkout -b <new-branch>: Creates a new branch and switches to it immediately.

4. Remote Synchronizationgit remote add origin <url>: Connects your local repository to a remote server.git push origin <branch>: Uploads your local commits to the remote repository.git pull: Fetches changes from the remote repository and immediately merges them into your local branch.git fetch: Downloads changes from the remote repository but does not merge them into your local files.

# create repository
git init

# check status
git status

# create file
touch app.py

# add file
git add app.py

# commit
git commit -m "Created app.py"

# create branch
git checkout -b feature-login

# merge changes
git checkout main
git merge feature-login

# push to GitHub
git push origin main


Best Practices
Beginners

✅ Commit often
✅ Use meaningful commit messages
✅ Pull before pushing
✅ Create branches for features

Advanced Users

✅ Use rebase carefully
✅ Protect main branch
✅ Use SSH authentication
✅ Review commits before pushing
